<%# ** THE RULES WHOSE SPENDING IS NOT COUNTED AGAINST THEM, and why each one. ** Local: `rules`
    (BudgetPagePresenter::Rule list).

    A band of its own, because the list above it is the GIVE-WAY ORDER and these are not in it:
    `Category.in_fill_order` is holders only, so a rule on a category with no "holding since" date
    cannot be ranked and cannot be dragged. Rendering them among the groups would draw a priority
    badge and two reorder arrows on a card `Category.apply_fill_order` refuses to include.

    ** WHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG WITH THEM HAS CHANGED, AND THE BAND SAYS THE NEW THING. ** Under the
    distribution these rules asked for money no waterfall would ever hand them — "nothing fills
    it". Under computed claims every rule claims (`ClaimLedger` counts all of them into `#free`,
    which is Task 3's ruling 9), so the claim is not the problem: the SPENDING is.
    `CategoryLedger::ENTRY_CATEGORY_ID` attributes an expense to its category only from
    `funded_since` onward, so a rule on a category with no such date claims its full amount every
    period while nothing the user spends there ever comes off it. That is money counted against
    them twice over, and one date fixes it.

    THEY ARE NOT HIDDEN, and that is the point of the band rather than a filter. Every rule here is
    inside `Budget.steady_need` and inside `ClaimLedger#total_claims`, so both the structural check
    below and Home's free figure count it: a user whose money is $500 short, over a list showing
    nothing, has to be able to see where the $500 went.

    Silent when there are none — an empty "Not in the fill order" heading invents a problem out of a
    healthy screen. `data-not-filling` rather than the groups' own selector, so a category a user
    named "Not in the fill order" cannot collide with it. %>
<%# WARNING, NOT DANGER (design review, nits). A rule outside the give-way order is a SETUP fact —
    a category that has not started counting its own spending yet, a rule the cutover left behind —
    and both are fixed by filling a field in. Red is this app's colour for money that is short or a bill that is
    late; spending it here put a band the user can resolve at leisure in the same register as an
    overdraft, and the two bands sat one above the other on the same screen.

    ITS OWN EYEBROW, matching `_fill_order`'s (design review, nits). Without one the card read as
    another item in the fill-order list — the very sequence its whole point is that it is not part
    of — because the list above it is introduced by a heading and this was not. Same shape, same
    two lines, so the two bands read as two sections rather than one list with a red row in it. %>
<% if rules.any? %>
  <section class="space-y-3" data-not-filling>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:justify-between gap-1 sm:gap-4 px-1">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Not in the give-way order</h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">Your spending here isn&rsquo;t counted against them — each row says why</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-white border border-status-warning rounded">
      <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 bg-status-warning-light">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-status-warning">
          <%= pluralize(rules.size, "rule") %> counting no spending
        </h3>
      </div>

      <% rules.each do |rule| %>
        <%# STACKS BELOW `sm` (design review H5), for the same reason the group cards do: the name
            plus a reason clause plus a figure plus two links is four things, and the reason is the
            longest of them. %>
        <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0"
             data-not-filling-rule="<%= budget_rule_name(rule.budget) %>">
          <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-between sm:items-baseline gap-1 sm:gap-4">
            <span class="font-medium text-gray-900">
              <%= budget_rule_name(rule.budget) %>
              <span class="text-xs font-normal text-status-warning">· <%= budget_rule_unfilled_reason(rule.budget) %></span>
            </span>
            <span class="text-sm text-gray-700 flex items-baseline gap-3 shrink-0">
              <span><%= budget_rule_amount(rule.budget) %></span>
              <%# TWO DOORS, AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY FIXES THIS (design review,
                  nits). "Edit" opens the RULE, and there is nothing wrong with the rule: the row's
                  own sentence says the CATEGORY is not counting its own spending yet, and the
                  field that changes that — "Claiming since" — is on the category form. A band that names a
                  cause and links only to the thing that is not the cause sends the user round a
                  loop. Rendered only where there is a category to edit; the cutover arm has none,
                  which is exactly what its own reason says. %>
              <%= link_to "Edit rule", edit_budget_path(rule.budget), class: "text-brand-dark hover:text-brand underline" %>
              <% if rule.budget.category.present? %>
                <%= link_to "Set a claiming date",
                            edit_category_path(rule.budget.category),
                            class: "text-brand-dark hover:text-brand underline",
                            data: { not_filling_category: rule.budget.category.name } %>
              <% end %>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>
<% end %>
